The '''Ginkgoaceae''' are a family of [[gymnosperm]]s which appeared during the [[Mesozoic Era]], of which the only extant representative is ''[[Ginkgo biloba]]'', which is for this reason sometimes regarded as a [[living fossil]]. Formerly, however, there were several other genera, and forests of ginkgo existed. Because leaves can take such diverse forms within a single species, these are a poor measure of diversity, although differing structures of wood point to the existence of diverse ginkgo forests in ancient times.<ref name="Gnaedinger2011">{{Cite journal | last1 = Gnaedinger | first1 = S. | doi = 10.1016/j.geobios.2011.01.007 | title = Ginkgoalean woods from the Jurassic of Argentina: Taxonomic considerations and palaeogeographical distribution | journal = Geobios | volume = 45 | issue = 2 | pages = 187–198 | year = 2011 | hdl = 11336/25674 | hdl-access = free }}</ref>

